1. View

A View is a virtual table created from the result of a SQL query. It does not store data itself (except materialized views in some databases); it displays data from underlying tables.

Syntax
CREATE VIEW employee_view AS
SELECT employee_id, name, salary
FROM employees;

Usage
SELECT * FROM employee_view;

Advantages
Hides complex queries
Improves security by exposing only selected columns
Promotes code reuse

Example
Instead of writing:

SELECT employee_id, name
FROM employees
WHERE department_id = 10;

repeatedly, create a view and query it directly.

2. Stored Procedure

A Stored Procedure is a precompiled collection of SQL statements stored in the database and executed as a single unit.

MySQL Example
DELIMITER //

CREATE PROCEDURE GetEmployeeById(IN empId INT)
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM employees
    WHERE employee_id = empId;
END //

DELIMITER ;

Execute =>
CALL GetEmployeeById(101);

Advantages
Better performance (precompiled)
Reduces network traffic
Encapsulates business logic
Improves security
Real-world Example

A banking procedure that:

Debits one account
Credits another account
Commits the transaction

All in a single database call.

Types of Parameters in MySQL
1. IN Parameter
Used to pass values into the procedure.
2. OUT Parameter
Used to return a value from the procedure.
3. INOUT Parameter
Acts as both input and output.


3. Trigger

A Trigger is a special stored program that automatically executes when a specific event occurs on a table.

Events:
INSERT
UPDATE
DELETE


MySQL Example
CREATE TRIGGER before_employee_insert
BEFORE INSERT
ON employees
FOR EACH ROW
SET NEW.created_date = NOW();

Audit Example
CREATE TRIGGER employee_audit
AFTER UPDATE
ON employees
FOR EACH ROW
INSERT INTO employee_log
VALUES (OLD.employee_id, NOW());

Advantages
Automatic execution
Audit logging
Data validation
Maintaining derived data


What is Normalization?

Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to:
Reduce data duplication (redundancy)
Improve data consistency

1NF (First Normal Form)
Rule:
Each column should contain only a single value.
No repeating groups.

❌ Bad
| StudentID | Courses      |
| --------- | ------------ |
| 1         | Java, Spring |


✅ Good
| StudentID | Course |
| --------- | ------ |
| 1         | Java   |
| 1         | Spring |


2NF (Second Normal Form)
Rule:
Must be in 1NF.
Every non-key column should depend on the entire primary key.

Suppose:
| StudentID | CourseID | StudentName |
| --------- | -------- | ----------- |
| 1         | 101      | John        |


Primary Key = (StudentID, CourseID)

Problem:
StudentName depends only on StudentID, not on the full key.

Split into:

Students
| StudentID | StudentName |
| --------- | ----------- |
| 1         | John        |


Enrollments
| StudentID | CourseID |
| --------- | -------- |
| 1         | 101      |

3NF (Third Normal Form)
Rule:
Must be in 2NF.
No transitive dependency.

❌ Bad
| EmpID | EmpName | DeptID | DeptName |
| ----- | ------- | ------ | -------- |
| 1     | John    | 10     | HR       |


Problem:
DeptName depends on DeptID, not directly on EmpID.
DeptName is indirectly depending on EmpID that is what called transitive dependency.This is exactly what 3NF removes.

Split into:

Employee
| EmpID | EmpName | DeptID |
| ----- | ------- | ------ |
| 1     | John    | 10     |

Department
| DeptID | DeptName |
| ------ | -------- |
| 10     | HR       |


Normalization is a database design technique used to eliminate data redundancy and maintain data integrity by splitting large tables into smaller related tables.

1NF: Remove repeating groups and ensure atomic values.
2NF: Remove partial dependencies.
3NF: Remove transitive dependencies.

One-Line Memory Trick
1NF: One value per cell.
2NF: Depend on the whole key.
3NF: Depend only on the key, not on another non-key column.
